  "prediction": "**Early Withdrawals of Retirement Savings: Incidence, Factors, and Strategies**\n\nThe Government Accountability Office (GAO) has released a report examining the incidence and factors contributing to early withdrawals of retirement savings from 401(k) plans and Individual Retirement Accounts (IRAs). The report highlights the significant impact of early withdrawals on retirement savings and identifies potential strategies to reduce this behavior.\n\n**Incidence of Early Withdrawals**\n\nThe report estimates that in 2013, individuals ages 25 to 55 withdrew approximately $68.7 billion from their retirement accounts, with IRA owners accounting for $39.5 billion of this amount. The majority of these withdrawals were due to hardship withdrawals, cashouts at job separation, and unrepaid plan loans. The report also notes that the incidence of early withdrawals is higher among certain demographic groups, including those with lower incomes, shorter job tenures, and those experiencing financial stress.\n\n**Factors Contributing to Early Withdrawals**\n\nThe report identifies several factors contributing to early withdrawals, including:\n\n1. Financial stress and limited emergency savings\n2. Limited financial literacy and understanding of retirement savings options\n3. Complexity of retirement savings rules and regulations\n4. Plan rules governing early participant withdrawals\n5. Economic shocks, such as job loss or medical emergencies\n\n**Strategies to Reduce Early Withdrawals**\n\nThe report suggests several strategies to reduce early withdrawals, including:\n\n1. Simplifying the rollover process for 401(k) plans\n2. Restricting cashouts to participant contributions only\n3. Narrowing the IRS safe harbor for hardship withdrawals\n4. Replacing hardship withdrawals with hardship loans\n5. Incorporating emergency savings features into 401(k) plans\n6. Providing financial education and counseling to participants\n7. Implementing plan policies to reduce loan defaults and hardship withdrawals\n\n**Recommendations**\n\nThe report recommends that the Secretary of Labor direct the Assistant Secretary for EBSA to revise the Form 5500 to require plan sponsors to report qualified plan loan offsets as a separate line item. This would provide better insight into the incidence and amount of loan offsets in 401(k) plans.\n\nOverall, the report highlights the importance of addressing early withdrawals of retirement savings to ensure the long-term security of retirement savings. By understanding the factors contributing to early withdrawals and implementing effective strategies to reduce this behavior, policymakers and plan sponsors can help individuals achieve their retirement goals.",
